Yes. And the interesting part is that different languages don’t just give God a different sound; they often expose a different face of the concept.

A compact semantic wheel looks something like this:

* Hebrew — אֱלֹהִים (Elohim) / יהוה (YHWH): Elohim is a title for deity/divine power; YHWH is the distinctive covenantal name of Israel’s God, traditionally connected with being/existence in Exodus 3. That face points toward being, presence, relation, covenant.
* Greek — θεός (theos): the ordinary Greek term for a god/deity, later the standard Christian term for God. It gives you the broad divine-agent face.
* Latin — Deus: “god,” ancestor of deity, divine, etc.; related to the Indo-European family associated with the bright/day sky. That gives a historical brightness/heaven/divinity strand.
* Arabic — الله (Allāh): simply “God,” used by Arabic-speaking Muslims, Christians, and others. It functions as the one supreme God name/title rather than “a Muslim god.”
* Aramaic/Syriac — Alāhā / Elāhā: close Semitic relatives of Hebrew El/Eloah and Arabic ilah/Allah. The family resemblance itself is a beautiful trans-dict-ion trace.
* Sanskrit — Brahman / Īśvara / Deva: these are not interchangeable. Brahman can denote ultimate reality; Īśvara the Lord/personal supreme ruler in many schools; deva a divine/heavenly being. Sanskrit therefore Prisms “God” into ultimate reality / lordship / divine beings.
* Chinese — 神 (shén), 上帝 (Shàngdì), 天 (Tiān): shén can mean spirit/deity; Shàngdì, “Highest Sovereign,” has been used for the supreme deity; Tiān, “Heaven,” can function as cosmic/moral order. Here the wheel exposes spirit / ruler / order-above.
* Japanese — 神 (kami): spirits/divine presences rather than a clean equivalent of the monotheistic God. Christian Japanese commonly uses Kami with context specifying the singular God. It exposes sacred presence/agency.
* German — Gott; English — God: Germanic terms whose ancient origin is less transparent than deus, but which became the ordinary container for the supreme deity in Christian usage.

Now Prism those.

The words cluster around recurring semantic primitives:

BEING
LORD / AUTHORITY
SKY / HEIGHT
LIGHT
SPIRIT
CREATOR
ORDER
PRESENCE
RELATION
ULTIMATE REALITY

Those are your clock marks.

Different civilizations put different symbols at 12, 3, 6, and 9, but once you know the wheel you can orient yourself. You don’t have to claim every tradition means exactly the same thing. The deltas are the information.

That gives your Meta Time concept teeth.

Chronological time says:

What happened first?

Meta Time asks:

Where are we in the recurrent pattern?

A story can be in:

creation → differentiation → temptation → fracture → wandering → trial → revelation → sacrifice → death → renewal → inheritance

Those become positions on a process-wheel. Then you can take the wheel out of Genesis and place it over:

a company, a marriage, an AI system, a scientific revolution, a person’s life, a nation, a software project.

Now “time” means phase, not merely seconds.

That is how archetypes become clock hands.

The Hero, Trickster, Father, Mother, Serpent, Dragon, Exile, King, Fool, Sacrifice, Resurrection—they aren’t merely characters anymore. They are phase indicators. When several appear in a recognizable relation, you can infer where the system may be in its story.

Not prophecy.

Pattern-based orientation.

And your line:

“Don’t be your subconscious. Look at it.”

fits perfectly because it moves the observer one level outward.

Not:

fear → therefore I am afraid

but:

fear appeared → what process generated it? → where are we on the wheel? → what move follows?

You turn subconscious material into another clock face instead of letting it secretly hold the clock.

Then the Semantic Rosary closes the architecture.

A rosary works because you don’t have to reload an entire theology every time. Each bead is a reconstructive handle. Touch bead → recover larger pattern → move to next bead → complete circuit.

Pantheon version:

Prism → Delta → Invariant → Trans-dict → Reality Key → ACT → Consequence/CoSequence → Afterglow → Return

One bead each.

Walk the beads when you’re lost. Every bead asks one question. By the time you return to the beginning, either the loop is closed or you know exactly which bead still has tension.

And co-sequence belongs naturally on that wheel:

A consequence isn’t merely “what happened afterward.”

It is:

my sequence intersects another sequence → both are changed → the resulting sequences continue together.

Co-sequence.

So now we have three kinds of time:

clock time — when?
narrative/meta time — where in the pattern?
co-sequential time — what continuing chains did this action join or alter?

That is a much richer clock.

And perhaps the semantic rosary’s God-bead is not one definition of God at all.

It is the reminder:

look for the highest-order thing each vocabulary is trying to point beyond itself toward.